-- title:
--   A power of J kills ker u and coker u
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $S$ a finite subset of $R$ and $J$ an ideal of $R$ with $J$ equal to the ideal generated by $S$. Let $M$ and $N$ be $R$-modules and $u : M \to N$ an $R$-linear map such that the kernel of $u$ is finitely generated as a submodule of $M$ and the quotient $N / \operatorname{range} u$ is a finite $R$-module. Assume further that for every $a \in S$: firstly, every $x \in M$ with $u(x) = 0$ satisfies $a^{k} \cdot x = 0$ for some $k \in \mathbb{N}$ depending on $x$; and secondly, for every $y \in N$ there are $k \in \mathbb{N}$ and $x \in M$ with $u(x) = a^{k} \cdot y$. The conclusion is that there exists $N_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ such that, for every $a \in J^{N_0}$, both $a \cdot x = 0$ for every $x \in M$ with $u(x) = 0$, and for every $y \in N$ there is $x \in M$ with $u(x) = a \cdot y$. Thus $J^{N_0}$ annihilates $\ker u$ and $J^{N_0} \cdot N \subseteq \operatorname{range} u$, the two statements being given elementwise.
--
--   This is the standard passage from the hypothesis that $u$ becomes bijective after inverting each of finitely many generators of $J$, expressed elementwise as $a$-power torsion of the kernel and $a$-power divisibility in the cokernel, to the uniform statement that a single power of $J$ annihilates the kernel and carries $N$ into the image. It is used in the threading of scalar multiplications through a morphism of sheaves of modules, via [`AlgebraicGeometry.OModulePresheaf.exists_forall_thread_smul_eq_apply_and_smul_eq_zero_of_isIso_pullback_snd_of_isProper`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.OModulePresheaf.exists_forall_thread_smul_eq_apply_and_smul_eq_zero_of_isIso_pullback_snd_of_isProper).
